        "text": "I\nI Finally, we\u2019re printing the value to the output screen using the printf\nfunction.\nTo execute it, make sure to give it the right permissions first (look at the fol-\nlowing output to see what happens if you don\u2019t). Since we\u2019re inside the same\ndirectory ( /root ), we will use ./var.sh to execute it:\nroot@kali:~# ./var.sh\nbash: ./var.sh: Permission denied\nroot@kali:~# chmod +x var.sh\nroot@kali:~# ./var.sh\nThe router IP address: 10.0.0.1\nCongratulations, you just built your first Bash script! Let\u2019s say we want this\nscript to run automatically without specifying its path anywhere in the system.\nTo do that, we must add it to the $PATH variable. In our case, we will add /opt\nto the $PATH variable so we can save our custom scripts in this directory.\nFirst, open the .bashrc file using any text editor. Once the file is loaded, scroll\nto the bottom and add the line highlighted in Figure 2.2.\nThe changes will append /opt to the $PATH variable. At this stage, save the file\nand close all the terminal sessions. Reopen the terminal window and copy the\nscript file to the /opt folder. From now on, we don\u2019t need to include its path; we",
